  "text": "To find all possible values of \\(c\\), we can use Vieta's formulas, which relate the coefficients of a polynomial to sums and products of its roots.\n\nFor \\(P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c\\), Vieta's formulas tell us:\n\\[\nr + s + t = -a,\n\\]\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = b,\n\\]\n\\[\nrst = -c.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(Q(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c + 120\\), the roots of this equation are \\(r-2\\), \\(s+5\\), and \\(t-1\\). Applying Vieta's formulas to \\(Q(x)\\), we find:\n\\[\n(r-2) + (s+5) + (t-1) = -a,\n\\]\n\\[\n(r-2)(s+5) + (r-2)(t-1) + (s+5)(t-1) = b,\n\\]\n\\[\n(r-2)(s+5)(t-1) = -(c + 120).\n\\]\n\nLet's start by simplifying these equations based on our known relationships from \\(P(x)\\).\n\nFor the sum of the roots:\n\\[(r-2) + (s+5) + (t-1) = -a.\\] Expanding this gives:\n\\[r + s + t + 2 = -a.\\] Since \\(r + s + t = -a\\) from \\(P(x)\\), this simplifies further to:\n\\[12 = -a,\\] so \\(a = -12.\\)\n\nNext, expanding the second sum equation:\n\\[(r-2)(s+5) + (r-2)(t-1) + (s+5)(t-1).\\] This expands to:\n\\[rs + 5r - 2s - 10 + rt - r - 2t + 2 + st + 5s - 5t - 25.\\] Simplifying, we get:\n\\[rs + rt + st + 3r + 3s - 3t - 43.\\]\nThis must equal \\(b\\), and since we already know \\(b = rs + rt + st\\) from \\(P(x)\\), this simplifies to:\n\\[b - 43 = rs + rt + st.\\] Therefore, \\(b = b - 43\\), which isn't surprising.\n\nFinally, expanding the product equation:\n\\[(r-2)(s+5)(t-1) = -(c + 120).\\] Expanding this follows:\n\\[r(s+5) - 2(s+5) + st - t = -(c + 120).\\] Simplifying, we get:\n\\[rst + 5(r - 2) - 2s - 10 + st - t = -c - 120.\\] Using \\(rst = -c\\) from \\(P(x)\\), and knowing \\(r + s + t = -a = 12\\), we substitute and solve:\n\\[-c - 12 + 5(r - 2) = -c - 120.\\] Simplifying further, we find:\n\\[5r - 10 = -108,\\] so \\(5r = -98\\), which gives \\(r = -\\frac{98}{5}\\). Substituting back, we solve for \\(c\\):\n\\[-c - 12 + 5(-\\frac{98}{5} - 2) = -c - 120.\\] Solving for \\(c\\):\n\\[-c - 12 - \\frac{490}{5} + 10 = -c - 120.\\] Simplifying, we find:\n\\[-c - \\frac{542}{5} + 10 = -c - 120.\\] This simplifies to \\(c = -120\\).\n\nThus, the possible value of \\(c\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{-124}.\\]verständlich",
